assertion  XSH.nearbyint.001
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       While the rounding direction is toward negative infinity, toward
           positive infinity or toward zero (each one that can be selected; at
           least one is needed), nearbyint() of -3.5, -2.75, -2.5, -1.5, -1.25,
           -0.75, 0.75, 1.25, 1.5, 2.5, 2.75 and 3.5 gives the next integer
           below, above or toward zero respectively.
test       t/001-directed.c

assertion  XSH.nearbyint.002
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       While the rounding direction is to nearest, halfway cases go to the
           even neighbour: nearbyint(0.5) is 0, nearbyint(1.5) is 2,
           nearbyint(2.5) is 2, nearbyint(3.5) is 4, nearbyint(4.5) is 4 and the
           negative counterparts; other values go to the nearest integer.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard). The test is NOTINUSE when to-nearest cannot be selected.
test       t/002-nearest-even.c

assertion  XSH.nearbyint.003
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       After the inexact flag is cleared, nearbyint(2.5), nearbyint(0.75) and
           nearbyint(-1.25) leave it clear.
test       t/003-no-inexact.c

assertion  XSH.nearbyint.004
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       In every selectable rounding direction, nearbyint(-0.75),
           nearbyint(-0.25), nearbyint(0.25) and nearbyint(0.75) have the same
           sign as the argument, even when the result is zero.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/004-sign.c

assertion  XSH.nearbyint.005
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       A NaN argument gives a NaN result.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/005-nan.c

assertion  XSH.nearbyint.006
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For +0 and -0 the result is the argument, with its sign.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/006-zero.c

assertion  XSH.nearbyint.007
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For +Inf and -Inf the result is the argument.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/007-infinity.c

assertion  XSH.nearbyint.008
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nearbyint.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      2001
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, nearbyint(2.5) leaves it
           unchanged and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the
           invalid, divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/008-no-error.c
